   513	// `=>` is unambiguous. A bare `=` is the Kotlin/Scala form, and it is read as an implementation
   514	// unless what follows is one of the three C++ special-member forms that assign no expression at
   515	// all: `= 0` (pure virtual), `= delete` and `= default`. Those remain declarations.
   516	//
   517	// The bias is deliberate. Treating an implementation as a declaration DEMOTES a fix site out of
   518	// the ranking; treating a declaration as an implementation merely leaves it at full weight, where
   519	// it competes on its score like anything else.
   520	func searchExpressionBodiedCallable(body string) bool {
   521		// Only what follows the SIGNATURE counts. A DEFAULT ARGUMENT is an `=` inside the parameter
   522		// list — `public function previous($fallback = false);` is a PHP interface method and nothing
   523		// else — so the scan starts after the parameter list closes, which is the first balanced paren
   524		// group. Not the LAST `)`: an expression body routinely ends in a call of its own, and
   525		// `fun formatAmount(value: Int) = value.toString()` would then be scanned from an empty tail.
   526		// A callable written without parentheses at all (Scala's `def f: Int = 42`) has none to skip,
   527		// and is scanned whole.
   528		tail := body
   529		if open := strings.IndexByte(body, '('); open >= 0 {
   530			depth := 0
   531			for index := open; index < len(body); index++ {
   532				switch body[index] {
   533				case '(':
   534					depth++
   535				case ')':
   536					depth--
   537				}
